const e="2025-english2",i=2025,t="英语二",a="2025年英语二",s=[{id:"sec-cloze",type:"cloze",title:"英语知识运用",directions:"Directions: Read the following text. Choose the best word(s) for each numbered blank and mark A, B, C or D on the ANSWER SHEET. (10 points)",articles:[{id:"cloze-1",title:"完形填空",type:"cloze",blocks:[{type:"paragraph",content:"There are many understandable reasons why you might find it difficult to ask for help when you need it. Psychologists have been interested in this "},{type:"blank",blankId:1},{type:"paragraph",content:" for decades, not least because people's widespread "},{type:"blank",blankId:2},{type:"paragraph",content:" to ask for help has led to some high-profile failures. Asking for help takes "},{type:"blank",blankId:3},{type:"paragraph",content:" . It involves communicating a need on your part—there's something you can't do. "},{type:"blank",blankId:4},{type:"paragraph",content:" you're broadcasting your own weaknesses, which can be "},{type:"blank",blankId:5},{type:"paragraph",content:" . You might worry about coming across as incompetent. You might have "},{type:"blank",blankId:6},{type:"paragraph",content:" about losing control of whatever it is you're asking for help with. "},{type:"blank",blankId:7},{type:"paragraph",content:" someone starts to help, perhaps they'll take over, or get credit for your earlier efforts. Yet another "},{type:"blank",blankId:8},{type:"paragraph",content:" that you might be worried about is being a nuisance or "},{type:"blank",blankId:9},{type:"paragraph",content:" the person you go to for help. If you struggle with low self-esteem, you might find it especially difficult to "},{type:"blank",blankId:10},{type:"paragraph",content:" for help because you have the added worry of the other person "},{type:"blank",blankId:11},{type:"paragraph",content:" your request. You might see such refusals as implying something "},{type:"blank",blankId:12},{type:"paragraph",content:" about the status of your relationship with them. To "},{type:"blank",blankId:13},{type:"paragraph",content:" these difficulties, try to remind yourself that everyone needs help sometimes. Nobody knows everything and can do everything all by themselves. And while you might "},{type:"blank",blankId:14},{type:"paragraph",content:" coming across as incompetent, there's actually research that shows that advice-seekers are "},{type:"blank",blankId:15},{type:"paragraph",content:" as more competent, not less. Perhaps most encouraging of all is a paper from 2022 by researchers at Stanford University that involved a mix of contrived help-seeking interactions and asking people to "},{type:"blank",blankId:16},{type:"paragraph",content:" times they'd sought help in the past. The findings showed that help-seekers generally underestimate how "},{type:"blank",blankId:17},{type:"paragraph",content:" other people will be to help and how good it'll make the help-giver feel (for most people, having the chance to help someone is highly "},{type:"blank",blankId:18},{type:"paragraph",content:" ). So, bear all this in mind the next time you need to ask for help. "},{type:"blank",blankId:19},{type:"paragraph",content:" take care over who you ask and when you ask them. And if someone can't help right now, avoid taking it personally. They might just be too "},{type:"blank",blankId:20},{type:"paragraph",content:" , or they might not feel confident about their ability to help."}]}],questions:[{id:1,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"illusion",B:"discussion",C:"tradition",D:"question"},answer:"D",analysis:'question 【线索】this 回指 "求助难" 这个值得研究的议题 线索就在前一句 There are many understandable reasons why you might find it difficult to ask for help when you need it.：作者把 "开口求助难" 当成一个值得心理学家 【选项】A) illusion ✗ 义偏 "错觉"，与所指不符 B) discussion ✗ 义偏 "讨论"，与研究对象不符 C) tradition ✗ 义偏 "传统"，对象错位 D) question ✓ "议题/问题" 与回指对象完全对位 议题，与 Psychologists have been interested in this __(1)__ for decades 完美对位。 【验证】代回后整句意为 Psychologists have been interested in this __(1)__ for decades——心',position:0},{id:2,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"reluctance",B:"ambition",C:"tendency",D:"enthusiasm"},answer:"A",analysis:`reluctance 【线索】求助难、且导致 high-profile failures，方向负面 前句 There are many understandable reasons why you might find it difficult to ask for help 已经定性 "难以求助"；本句又说 people's widespread __(2)__ to ask for help has led to some high-profile failures，明确这种倾向引发了重大失败。线索指向 "不愿/ 【选项】 A) reluctance ✓ "不情愿" 精准对应 "求助难" reluctance 意为 "不情愿、勉强"，与上文 find it difficult to ask for help 的负面倾向完全一 致，且能合理引发 high-profile failures。 B) ambition ✗ "雄心" 极性正向，方向反 C) tendency ✗ 中性 "倾向"，但与 to ask for help 搭配后方向反 tendency 本身中性，但 tendency to ask for help 表示 "倾向于求助"，与原文 "难以求助" 方向 D) enthusiasm ✗ "热情" 极性正向，方向反 【验证】代入后 people's widespread __(2)__ to ask for help has led to some high-profile`,position:0},{id:3,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"attention",B:"talent",C:"courage",D:"patience"},answer:"C",analysis:`courage 【线索】下文讲暴露弱点、怕显得无能 话题句 Asking for help takes __(3)__. 之后紧跟 It involves communicating a need on your part—there's something you can't do. 和 you're broadcasting your own weaknesses, which can be __(5)__.——求助意味着暴露弱点、承认自己做不到，这是需要 【选项】A) attention ✗ "注意力" 与暴露弱点无关 B) talent ✗ "天赋" 与心理障碍无关 C) courage ✓ "勇气" 与暴露弱点正相关 courage 与下文 you're broadcasting your own weaknesses 形成因果——正因为要公开弱 点，才需要勇气；这是英语中 "It takes courage to do sth." 的经典搭配。 D) patience ✗ "耐心" 与瞬时心理障碍不符 【验证】代入后整段逻辑通畅：Asking for help takes __(3)__. 求助需要勇气，因为它会让你暴露弱`,position:0},{id:4,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"At any time",B:"In other words",C:"By all means",D:"On the contrary"},answer:"B",analysis:`In other words 【线索】前后两句是同义改写关系 前句 It involves communicating a need on your part—there's something you can't do. 说求助等于承认自己有做不到的事；本句 __(4)__ you're broadcasting your own 【选项】A) At any time ✗ 时间义、与逻辑关系无关 B) In other words ✓ "换句话说" 精准对应同义复述 C) By all means ✗ "务必" 语义错位 D) On the contrary ✗ "恰恰相反"，方向反 On the contrary 表转折，但前后两句并无对立，是同义而非反义。 【验证】代入后 __(4)__ you're broadcasting your own weaknesses, which can be __(5)__. 衔接`,position:0},{id:5,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"unrealistic",B:"deceptive",C:"tiresome",D:"uncomfortable"},answer:"D",analysis:`uncomfortable 【线索】暴露弱点带来心理不适 you're broadcasting your own weaknesses, which can be __(5)__. 中 which 指 "广播自 己的弱点"，后文 You might worry about coming across as incompetent. 进一步描述这 【选项】A) unrealistic ✗ "不切实际"、对象错位 B) deceptive ✗ "欺骗性"，对象错位 C) tiresome ✗ "令人厌烦"，重心偏疲倦感 D) uncomfortable ✓ "令人不适" 与心理负担吻合 uncomfortable 表 "令人不舒服的、不安的"，与紧接其后的 You might worry about coming across as incompetent. 形成自然衔接。 【验证】代入后 you're broadcasting your own weaknesses, which can be __(5)__. 表达 "暴露弱`,position:0},{id:6,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"doubts",B:"concerns",C:"suggestions",D:"secrets"},answer:"B",analysis:`concerns 【线索】about 后接 "失去控制" 是负面顾虑 You might have __(6)__ about losing control of whatever it is you're asking for help with. 后接 losing control 这种典型负面情境；前句 You might worry about coming across as incompetent. 已经定调为 "worry" 一类的担忧。线索指向 "担忧、顾虑" 这种情 【选项】A) doubts ✗ "怀疑"，搭配偏 doubt about sth 是否真实 B) concerns ✓ "担忧" 与 worry 同义接力 concerns 即 "忧虑、关切的事"，与前句 worry about 完美呼应，且 have concerns about 是 C) suggestions ✗ "建议"，情感方向错 D) secrets ✗ "秘密"，语义错位 secrets 与 about losing control 搭配不通——"对失控有秘密" 既不像英语表达也无原文依据。 【验证】代回后 You might have __(6)__ about losing control of whatever it is you're asking for help with. 即 "你可能担心失去对所求助之事的控制"，与前后两句的 worry/担忧 链条`,position:0},{id:7,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"Once",B:"Unless",C:"Although",D:"Before"},answer:"A",analysis:`Once 【线索】他人开始帮忙是 "被接管/抢功" 的前提 __(7)__ someone starts to help, perhaps they'll take over, or get credit for your 生 "接管" 或 "抢功"。 【选项】 A) Once ✓ "一旦…就" 正好衔接条件—结果 B) Unless ✗ "除非"，方向反 C) Although ✗ "尽管"，转折与原文不符 D) Before ✗ "在…之前" 时序倒置 【验证】代入后 __(7)__ someone starts to help, perhaps they'll take over 表达 "一旦有人开始帮`,position:0},{id:8,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"theory",B:"choice",C:"factor",D:"context"},answer:"C",analysis:`factor 【线索】前文列举的都是 "让你担心的顾虑因素" 前文已列 You might worry about coming across as incompetent.、You might have __(6)__ about losing control、__(7)__ someone starts to help, perhaps they'll take 【选项】A) theory ✗ "理论"，类别错位 B) choice ✗ "选择"，对象不符 choice 表 "选择"，但 "被接管"、"显得无能" 不是 "选择"，是不可控的顾虑。 C) factor ✓ "因素" 准确概括前文列举 D) context ✗ "语境/背景"，搭配方向错 context 指 "上下文/背景"，不能用 worried about a context 描述心理顾虑。 【验证】代回后 Yet another __(8)__ that you might be worried about is being a nuisance 衔接`,position:0},{id:9,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"overpraising",B:"outperforming",C:"reassessing",D:"inconveniencing"},answer:"D",analysis:'inconveniencing 【线索】前并列项是 "being a nuisance" Yet another __(8)__ that you might be worried about is being a nuisance or __(9)__ the person you go to for help. 中 or 前并列项是 "being a nuisance（成为麻烦/讨厌 麻烦"。 【选项】A) overpraising ✗ "过度赞美"，方向反 overpraising 是过度夸奖，正向行为，与 nuisance 方向相反。 B) outperforming ✗ "表现胜过"，对象错位 outperforming 指 "业绩胜过某人"，与 "怕给求助对象添麻烦" 无关。 C) reassessing ✗ "重新评估"，对象错位 reassessing 是 "重新评估"，与本句心理顾虑无关。 D) inconveniencing ✓ "给…添麻烦" 与 nuisance 同义并列 inconveniencing 即 "给某人带来不便"，与 being a nuisance 形成同义并列，且宾语正是 the person。 【验证】代回后 being a nuisance or __(9)__ the person you go to for help 意为 "成为讨厌鬼、',position:0},{id:10,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"reach out",B:"settle down",C:"turn over",D:"look back"},answer:"A",analysis:'reach out 【线索】段落主题 "求助难"、空后 for help you might find it especially difficult to __(10)__ for help 主题就是 "开口求助"，且空后 紧跟 for help。reach out for help 是 "伸手求助" 的固定搭配。本空只需看本句即可定。 【选项】 A) reach out ✓ reach out for help 固定搭配 reach out 与 for help 构成固定搭配 "reach out for help"——伸手求助，正是本段主题。 B) settle down ✗ "安顿/平静"，搭配错 settle down 表 "安顿下来"，不与 for help 搭配。 C) turn over ✗ "翻转/移交"，搭配错 turn over 是 "翻转/移交"，不与 for help 搭配。 D) look back ✗ "回顾"，搭配错 look back 表 "回顾过去"，与 for help 不搭。 【验证】代回后 you might find it especially difficult to __(10)__ for help 意为 "你会发现尤其难',position:0},{id:11,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"declining",B:"considering",C:"criticising",D:"evaluating"},answer:"A",analysis:'declining 【线索】下文 such refusals 直接定性 紧随其后的 You might see such refusals as implying something __(12)__ about the status of your relationship with them. 用 such refusals 回指上句的动作。线索锁死答案 【选项】 A) declining ✓ "拒绝" 与 such refusals 完美对应 declining 表 "婉拒、拒绝"，正好与下文 such refusals 形成名动呼应。 B) considering ✗ "考虑"，与 refusals 不符 considering 是 "考虑"，并非拒绝，与下文 such refusals 矛盾。 C) criticising ✗ "批评"，对象偏 criticising 表 "批评"，是对内容的负面评价，但 "批评你的请求" 不等于 "拒绝"，与 such refusals D) evaluating ✗ "评估"，中性、不等于拒绝 【验证】代回后 you have the added worry of the other person __(11)__ your request 意为 "还',position:0},{id:12,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"unnecessary",B:"negative",C:"strange",D:"impractical"},answer:"B",analysis:'negative 【线索】整段在讲低自尊的负面解读 If you struggle with low self-esteem, you might find it especially difficult to __(10)__ for help 已定调本段聚焦 "低自尊→负面解读"；下一句 To __(13)__ these difficulties, try to remind yourself that everyone needs help sometimes. 把这些解读统称为 difficulties。线索指向 "负面的"。 【选项】A) unnecessary ✗ "不必要的"，无关 unnecessary 表 "不必要"，与关系状况的好坏无关。 B) negative ✓ "负面的" 与低自尊推断方向一致 C) strange ✗ "奇怪的"，方向不准 D) impractical ✗ "不实用的"，对象错位 【验证】代回后 You might see such refusals as implying something __(12)__ about the status of your relationship with them. 意为 "你会把拒绝看作暗示你们关系出了什么负面问题"，',position:0},{id:13,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"explain",B:"identify",C:"predict",D:"overcome"},answer:"D",analysis:'overcome 【线索】后接的是消除负面想法的具体建议 To __(13)__ these difficulties, try to remind yourself that everyone needs help sometimes. 后给出的是 "提醒自己人人都需要帮助" 这样消解 difficulties 的方法，与 "克 【选项】A) explain ✗ "解释"，与解决方法不匹配 explain 是 "解释"，后续给的不是解释 difficulties 的来源，而是消解它。 B) identify ✗ "识别"，方向不彻底 C) predict ✗ "预测"，对象错位 predict 是 "预测"，与本句给出的 "应对建议" 方向无关。 D) overcome ✓ overcome difficulties 固定搭配且贴合方向 overcome 与 difficulties 是高频固定搭配 "克服困难"，且与下文给出的实操建议完全自洽。 【验证】代回后 To __(13)__ these difficulties, try to remind yourself that everyone needs help',position:0},{id:14,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"deny",B:"forget",C:"miss",D:"fear"},answer:"D",analysis:`fear 【线索】while…there's actually research 的让步反转 while you might __(14)__ coming across as incompetent, there's actually research that shows that advice-seekers are __(15)__ as more competent, not less. while 引导 【选项】A) deny ✗ "否认"，与担心不同 B) forget ✗ "忘记"，与情感方向不符 forget 表 "忘记"，与心理顾虑无关。 C) miss ✗ "错过/想念"，对象错位 D) fear ✓ "害怕" 与 worry 情感链接力 fear 表 "害怕"，与前文反复出现的 worry about coming across as incompetent 同向同质， 【验证】代回后 while you might __(14)__ coming across as incompetent, there's actually research that shows that advice-seekers are __(15)__ as more competent, not less. 意`,position:0},{id:15,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"disguised",B:"perceived",C:"followed",D:"introduced"},answer:"B",analysis:'perceived 【线索】研究结果是 "别人怎么看求助者" advice-seekers are __(15)__ as more competent, not less 表达的是研究中别人对求助者 【选项】A) disguised ✗ "伪装成"，方向错 disguised 表 "伪装成"，与 "被认为" 无关，且 disguised as more competent 不通顺。 B) perceived ✓ be perceived as 标准搭配 perceived 表 "被视为"，be perceived as more competent 即 "被认为更能干"，与 research C) followed ✗ "跟随"，搭配错 followed 是 "跟随"，followed as more competent 不通。 D) introduced ✗ "被介绍为"，搭配偏 【验证】代回后 advice-seekers are __(15)__ as more competent, not less 意为 "求助者反而被视',position:0},{id:16,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"recall",B:"classify",C:"analyse",D:"compare"},answer:"A",analysis:`recall 【线索】the past + 让被试做的事 asking people to __(16)__ times they'd sought help in the past 关键在 times they'd sought help in the past——线索指向 "回忆过去经历"。 【选项】 A) recall ✓ "回忆" 与 in the past 完美对位 B) classify ✗ "分类"，与单次记忆不符 C) analyse ✗ "分析"，与方法描述不符 D) compare ✗ "比较"，对象不齐 compare 表 "比较"，需要两组对象，但本句宾语只是 times they'd sought help，不是对比 【验证】代回后 asking people to __(16)__ times they'd sought help in the past 意为 "让人们回`,position:0},{id:17,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"brave",B:"disappointed",C:"willing",D:"hesitant"},answer:"C",analysis:`willing 【线索】研究反转主旨：他人比想象中乐意帮忙 The findings showed that help-seekers generally underestimate how __(17)__ other people will be to help and how good it'll make the help-giver feel 与下文 having the chance to help someone is highly __(18)__ 共同指明研究反转方向：人们 "低估了" 别人 【选项】A) brave ✗ "勇敢"，对象不符 B) disappointed ✗ "失望"，方向反 disappointed 是负面词，与 underestimate how + 形容词 的正面属性结构相悖。 C) willing ✓ "愿意的" 完美对位反转结论 willing 表 "愿意的"，与 be willing to help 是标准搭配，研究反转结论正是 "他人比你想象中愿 D) hesitant ✗ "犹豫的"，方向反 hesitant 是负面词，按 underestimate 反向推理也不对——"低估别人有多犹豫" 不构成令人鼓舞 【验证】代回后 help-seekers generally underestimate how __(17)__ other people will be to`,position:0},{id:18,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"relaxing",B:"surprising",C:"rewarding",D:"demanding"},answer:"C",analysis:`rewarding 【线索】括号外 how good 决定方向 前文 how good it'll make the help-giver feel 已经定调帮助他人 "让助人者感觉很好"，括 号内 having the chance to help someone is highly __(18)__ 是对其进一步具体化。线索 【选项】A) relaxing ✗ "放松的"，方向偏 B) surprising ✗ "令人惊讶"，方向偏 surprising 表 "令人意外"，可解释 finding 出人意料，但不能直接形容 having the chance to help 本身的属性。 C) rewarding ✓ "有回报的" 与 feel good 同向 rewarding 表 "有成就感的、值得做的"，与 how good it'll make the help-giver feel 完美 D) demanding ✗ "要求高的"，方向反 【验证】代回后 having the chance to help someone is highly __(18)__ 意为 "有机会帮人是非常`,position:0},{id:19,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"Thus",B:"Also",C:"Finally",D:"Instead"},answer:"B",analysis:`Also 【线索】前句给总建议、本句给附加细则 前句 So, bear all this in mind the next time you need to ask for help. 是总建议；本句 __(19)__ take care over who you ask and when you ask them. 给出附加的 "选谁问、什 么时候问" 细则；后面又接着 And if someone can't help right now, avoid taking it 【选项】A) Thus ✗ "因此"，因果关系不成立 B) Also ✓ "另外" 与并列累加吻合 C) Finally ✗ "最后"，与后文还有一条建议矛盾 Finally 应放在最后一条建议前，但本句后面还有 And if someone can't help right now, avoid taking it personally.，并未结束。 D) Instead ✗ "取而代之"，与前句无对立 【验证】代回后 __(19)__ take care over who you ask and when you ask them. 衔接顺畅——"另`,position:0},{id:20,articleId:"cloze-1",type:"cloze",question:"",options:{A:"polite",B:"proud",C:"busy",D:"lazy"},answer:"C",analysis:`busy 【线索】right now + or 平行项的善意基调 And if someone can't help right now, avoid taking it personally. They might just be too __(20)__, or they might not feel confident about their ability to help. 中 right now 暗示时间不凑巧；后并列项 they might not feel confident about their ability to 【选项】A) polite ✗ "礼貌"，方向错 B) proud ✗ "骄傲"，与本段善意基调不符 proud 表 "骄傲"，与作者劝慰口吻不符，且 "太骄傲" 不能解释 right now 拒帮。 C) busy ✓ "忙" 与 right now 完美对位 D) lazy ✗ "懒"，色彩责备、违背基调 【验证】代回后 They might just be too __(20)__, or they might not feel confident about their OVERVIEW · 通读建境 全文是一篇心理科普短文，主旨可由开篇 There are many understandable reasons why you might find it difficult to ask for help when you need it. 一句定调：作者承认 "开口求 been interested in this __(1)__ for decades；第 2 段集中拆解 "求助难" 的多重恐惧（暴露弱 点、失去控制、给人添麻烦）Asking for help takes __(3)__.；第 3 段把低自尊群体单列出来 加码 If you struggle with low self-esteem, you might find it especially difficult to __(10)__ for help；第 4 段用 2022 年斯坦福研究做关键反转 The findings showed that help-seekers generally underestimate how __(17)__ other people will be to help；第 5 段给出最终行动 建议 So, bear all this in mind the next time you need to ask for help.。 SENTENCE INSIGHTS · 句子精讲 12 There are many understandable reasons why you might find it difficult to ask for help when you need it. Psychologists have been interested in this question for decades, not least because people's widespread reluctance to ask for help has led to some high-profile failures. （"reluctance to ask for help"）和现在完成时的使用都是常见考点，具有较强的代表性和迁移 In other words you're broadcastin...`,position:0}]},{id:"sec-reading",type:"reading",title:"阅读理解",directions:"Read the following four texts. Answer the questions after each text by choosing A, B, C or D. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET. (40 points)",articles:[{id:"text-1",title:"Text 1",type:"reading",blocks:[{type:"paragraph",content:"U.S. customers historically tipped people they assumed were earning most of their income via tips, such as restaurant servers earning less than the minimum wage. In the early 2010s, a wide range of businesses started processing purchases with iPads and other digital payment systems. These systems often prompted customers to tip for services that were not previously tipped. Today's tip requests are often not connected to the salary and service norms that used to determine when and how people tip. Customers in the past nearly always paid tips after receiving a service, such as at the conclusion of a restaurant meal, after getting a haircut or once a pizza was delivered. That timing could reward high-quality service and give workers an incentive to provide it. It's becoming more common for tips to be requested beforehand. And new tipping technology may even automatically add tips. The prevalence of digital payment devices has made it easier to ask customers for a tip. That helps explain why tip requests are creeping into new kinds of services. Customers now routinely see menus of suggested default options—often well above 20% of what they owe. The amounts have risen from 10% or less in the 1950s to 15% around the year 2000 to 20% or higher today. This increase is sometimes called tipflation—the expectation of ever-higher tip amounts. Tipping has always been a vital source of income for workers in historically tipped services, like restaurants, where the tipped minimum wage can be as low as US$2.13 an hour. Tip creep and tipflation are now further supplementing the income of many low-wage service workers. Notably, tipping primarily benefits some of these workers, such as waiters, but not others, such as cooks and dishwashers. To ensure that all employees were paid fair wages, some restaurants banned tipping and increased prices, but this movement toward no-tipping services has largely fizzled out. So, to increase employee wages without raising prices, more employers are succumbing to the temptations of tip creep and tipflation. However, many customers are frustrated because they feel they are being asked for too high of a tip, too often. And, as our research emphasizes, tipping now seems to be more coercive, less generous and often completely dissociated from service quality."}]},{id:"text-2",title:"Text 2",type:"reading",blocks:[{type:"paragraph",content:'When it was established, the National Health Service (NHS) was visionary: offering high-quality, timely care to meet the dominant needs of the population it served. Nearly 75 years on, with the UK facing very different health challenges, it is clear that model is out of date. From life expectancy to cancer and infant mortality rates, we are lagging behind many of our peers. With more than 6.8 million on waitlists, healthcare is becoming increasingly inaccessible for those who cannot opt to pay for private treatment; and the cost of providing healthcare is increasingly squeezing out investment in other public services. As demand for healthcare continues to grow, pressures on the workforce—which is already near breaking point—will only become more acute. Many of the answers to the crisis in health and care are well rehearsed. We need to be much better at reducing and diverting demand on health services, rather than simply managing it. Much more needs to be invested in communities and primary care to reduce our reliance on hospitals. And capacity in social care needs to be greater, to support the growing number of people living with long-term conditions. Yet despite two decades of strategies and a number of major health reforms, we have failed to make meaningful progress on any of these aims. That is why the Reform think tank is launching a new programme of work entitled "Reimagining health", supported by ten former health ministers. Together, we are calling for a much more open and honest conversation about the future of health in the UK, and an "urgent rethink" of the hospital-centric model we retain. This must begin with the question of how we maximise the health of the nation, rather than "fix" the NHS. It is estimated, for example, that healthcare accounts for only about 20% of health outcomes. Much more important are the places we live, work and socialise—yet there is no clear cross-government strategy for improving these social determinants of health. Worse, when policies like the national obesity strategy are scrapped, taxpayers are left with the heavy price tag of treating the illnesses, like diabetes, that result. Reform wants to ask how power and resources should be distributed in our health system. What health functions should remain at the centre, and what should be given to local leaders, often responsible for services that create health, and with a much better understanding of the needs of their populations?'}]},{id:"text-3",title:"Text 3",type:"reading",blocks:[{type:"paragraph",content:`Heat action plans, or HAPs, have been proliferating in India in the past few years. In general, an HAP spells out when and how officials should issue heat warnings and alert hospitals and other institutions. Nagpur's plan, for instance, calls for hospitals to set aside "cold wards" in the summer for treating heatstroke patients, and advises builders to give construction laborers a break from work on very hot days. But implementation of existing HAPs has been uneven, according to a report from the Centre for Policy Research. Many lack adequate funding, it found. And their triggering thresholds often are not customized to the local climate. In some areas, high daytime temperatures alone might serve as an adequate trigger for alerts. But in other places, nighttime temperatures or humidity might be as important a gauge of risk as daytime highs. Mumbai's April heatstroke deaths highlighted the need for more nuanced and localized warnings, researchers say. That day's high temperature of roughly 36°C was 1°C shy of the heat wave alert threshold for coastal cities set by national meteorological authorities. But the effects of the heat were amplified by humidity—an often neglected factor in heat alert systems—and the lack of shade at the late-morning outdoor ceremony. To help improve HAPs, urban planner Rajashree Kotharkar's team is working on a model plan that outlines best practices and could be adapted to local conditions. Among other things, she says, all cities should create a vulnerability map to help focus responses on the populations most at risk. Such mapping doesn't need to be complex, Kotharkar says. "A useful map can be created by looking at even a few key parameters." For example, neighborhoods with a large elderly population or informal dwellings that cope poorly with heat could get special warnings or be bolstered with cooling centers. The Nagpur project has already created a risk and vulnerability map, which enabled Kotharkar to tell officials which neighborhoods to focus on in the event of a heat wave this summer. HAPs shouldn't just include short-term emergency responses, researchers say, but also recommend medium- to long-term measures that could make communities cooler. In Nagpur, for example, Kotharkar's team has been able to advise city officials about where to plant trees to provide shade. HAPs could also guide efforts to retrofit homes or modify building regulations. "Reducing deaths in an emergency is a good target to have, but it's the lowest target," says climate researcher Chandni Singh.`}]},{id:"text-4",title:"Text 4",type:"reading",blocks:[{type:"paragraph",content:"Navigating beyond the organised pavements and parks of our urban spaces, desire paths are the unofficial footprints of a community, revealing the unspoken preferences, shared shortcuts and collective choices of humans. Often appearing as trodden dirt tracks through otherwise neat green spaces, these routes of collective disobedience cut corners, bisect lawns and cross hills, representing the natural capability of people (and animals) to go from point A to point B most effectively. Urban planners interpret desire paths as more than just convenient shortcuts; they offer valuable insights into the dynamics between planning and behaviour. Ohio State University allowed its students to navigate the Oval, a lawn in the centre of campus, freely, then proceeded to pave the desire paths, creating a web of effective routes students had established. Yet, reluctance persists among other planners to integrate desire paths into formal plans, citing concerns about safety, environmental impact, or primarily, aesthetics. A Reddit webpage devoted to the phenomenon, boasting nearly 50,000 members, showcases images of local desire paths adorned with signs instructing pedestrians to adhere to designated walkways, underscoring the rebellious nature inherent in these human-made tracks. This clash highlights an ongoing struggle between the organic, user-driven evolution of public spaces and the desire for a visually curated and controlled urban environment. The Wickquasgeck Trail is an example of a historical desire path, created by Native Americans to cross the forests of Manhattan and move between settlements quickly. This trail, when Dutch colonists arrived, was widened and made into one of the main trade roads across the island, known at the time as de Heere Straat, or Gentlemen's Street. Following the British assumption of control in New York, the street was renamed Broadway. Notably, Broadway stands out as one of the few areas in NYC that defies the grid-based system applied to the rest of the city, cutting a diagonal across parts of the city. In online spaces, desire paths have sparked a fascination that can approach obsession, with the Reddit page serving as a hub. Contributors offer a wide array of stories, from little-known new shortcuts to long-established alternate routes. Animal desire paths, such as ducks forging trails through frozen ponds or dogs carving direct routes in gardens, highlight the adaptability of these trails in both human and animal experiences. As desire paths criss- cross through both physical and virtual landscapes, they stand as a proof of the collective insistence on forging unconventional routes and embracing the spirit of communal choice."}]}],questions:[{id:21,articleId:"text-1",type:"reading",question:"According to Paragraph 1, the practice of tipping in the U.S. ________.",options:{A:"was regarded as a sign of generosity",B:"was considered essential for waiters",C:"was a way of rewarding diligence",D:"was optional in most businesses"},answer:"B",analysis:"【判型】题干According to Paragraph 1, the practice of tipping in the U.S.显式指段，问的是该 段对小费做法的客观陈述，不带 infer / suggest / imply。 【拆句】According to Paragraph 1, the practice of tipping in the U.S. ________. 主句是 the practice of tipping in the U.S. + 空格谓语；According to Paragraph 1 是状语，限定信 息来源；选项是对主语 the practice of tipping 的谓语补全，理解上无陷阱。 【定位】P1 U.S. customers historically tipped people they assumed were earning most of their income via tips, such as restaurant servers earning less than the minimum wage. 【选项】A) 无中生有 B) ✓ 正确 earning most of their income via tips ↔ was considered essential for waiters U.S. customers historically tipped ↔ was considered 预判“小费对低薪服务员是必需收入”，四个选项中只有was considered essential for waiters准确对应“essential（必需）+ waiters（服务员）”，故锁 B。 was a way of rewarding diligence（奖励勤勉）对应的是reward high-quality 的Paragraph 1不符；且 diligence（勤勉）≠ high-quality service（优质服务）。 工种里给小费几乎是惯例、并非可有可无；was optional in most businesses反过来说 businesses 范围被放大。",position:0},{id:22,articleId:"text-1",type:"reading",question:"Compared with tips in the past, today's tips ________.",options:{A:"are paid much less frequently",B:"are less often requested in advance",C:"have less to do with service quality",D:"contribute less to workers' income"},answer:"C",analysis:"【判型】题干Compared with tips in the past, today's tips要求找两个时点之间的差异，是对原文 【拆句】Compared with tips in the past, today's tips ________. Compared with tips in the past 是过去分词短语作状语，逻辑主语是 today's tips；主语 today's tips 后接空缺谓语，由选 【定位】P2 Today's tip requests are often not connected to the salary and service norms that used to determine when and how people tip. Customers in the past nearly always paid tips after receiving a service, such as at the conclusion of a restaurant meal, after getting a haircut or once a pizza was delivered. That timing could reward high-quality service and give workers an incentive to provide it. 【选项】A) 无中生有 creeping into new kinds of services暗示场合更多了。are paid much less frequently在频率维度上无原文支撑。 原文It's becoming more common for tips to be requested beforehand明确说“事前 often requested in advance恰好把方向写反。 C) ✓ 正确 Today's tip requests are often not connected to the salary and service norms ↔ have less to do with service quality That timing could reward high-quality service ↔ service quality service quality精确命中这一维度，故锁 C。 原文Tip creep and tipflation are now further supplementing the income of many low-wage service workers明确说今天的小费正在“更多地”补贴低薪工人的收入； contribute less to workers' income反向缩小其贡献，与原文相反。",position:0},{id:23,articleId:"text-1",type:"reading",question:"Tip requests are creeping into new kinds of services as a result of ________.",options:{A:"the advancement of technology",B:"the desire for income increase",C:"the diversification of business",D:"the emergence of tipflation"},answer:"A",analysis:"A) 技术的进步 B) 增加收入的愿望 C) 业务的多元化 D) tipflation的出现 【判型】题干as a result of问的是已被原文用 helps explain why 这类显式因果句写出的原因，属于 【拆句】Tip requests are creeping into new kinds of services as a result of ________. 主句 Tip requests are creeping into new kinds of services 用现在进行时表趋势；as a result of 【定位】P4 The prevalence of digital payment devices has made it easier to ask customers for a tip. That helps explain why tip requests are creeping into new kinds of services. 【选项】A) ✓ 正确 The prevalence of digital payment devices ↔ the advancement of technology has made it easier to ask customers for a tip ↔ the advancement of technology the desire for income increase（加薪诉求）对应的是第 7 段to increase employee wages without raising prices, more employers are succumbing to the temptations of tip creep and tipflation，那是雇主“屈从于”小费蔓延的原因，而非小 原文从未提到“商业多元化 / 业态多元化”导致小费请求扩散；the diversification of business是无中生有的因。 原文This increase is sometimes called tipflation把 tipflation 定义为“小费比例不断上 tipflation把结果当原因，因果倒置。",position:0},{id:24,articleId:"text-1",type:"reading",question:"The movement toward no-tipping services was intended to ________.",options:{A:"promote consumption",B:"enrich income sources",C:"maintain reasonable prices",D:"guarantee income fairness"},answer:"D",analysis:"【判型】原文用 To ensure that ... 直接交代了该运动的目的，题干intended to问的就是这一显式目 【拆句】The movement toward no-tipping services was intended to ________. 主语 The movement toward no-tipping services 后接被动谓语 was intended to + 不定式（由选 【定位】P6 To ensure that all employees were paid fair wages, some restaurants banned tipping and increased prices, but this movement toward no-tipping services has largely fizzled out. 【选项】A) 无中生有 enrich income sources（丰富收入来源）问的是收入“多样化”，但原文目的是“公平”而 maintain reasonable prices与原文做法相反。 D) ✓ 正确 To ensure that all employees were paid fair wages ↔ guarantee income fairness all employees ↔ income fairness 预判目的是“工资公平”，四选项中 D 直接用“guarantee income fairness”复刻原文 ensure...fair wages，是最直接的同义替换，故锁 D。",position:0},{id:25,articleId:"text-1",type:"reading",question:"It can be learned from the last paragraph that tipping ________.",options:{A:"is becoming a burden for customers",B:"helps encourage quality service",C:"is vital to business development",D:"reflects the need to reduce prices"},answer:"A",analysis:"【判型】题干It can be learned from the last paragraph that tipping含 It can be learned 【拆句】It can be learned from the last paragraph that tipping ________. It 为形式主语，that 引 导真正主语从句 tipping ________；从句主语 tipping 后接空缺谓语（由选项补全）；can 【定位】P7 However, many customers are frustrated because they feel they are being asked for too high of a tip, too often. And, as our research emphasizes, tipping now seems to be more coercive, less generous and often completely dissociated from service quality. 【选项】A) ✓ 正确 many customers are frustrated because they feel they are being asked for too high of a tip, too often ↔ is becoming a burden for customers tipping now seems to be more coercive, less generous ↔ is becoming a burden for customers 预判方向是“小费成顾客负担”，四选项中只有 A 用“burden for customers”准确接住末 段对顾客 frustrated + coercive 的情绪 / 评价聚合，故锁 A。 原文often completely dissociated from service quality明确说小费“已与服务质量完 全脱钩”，自然不再激励优质服务；helps encourage quality service与该结论相反。 末段提的是“to increase employee wages without raising prices”——只是雇主不想 涨价，并未上升到“需要降价”的结论；reflects the need to reduce prices在“不涨”基础 SENTENCE INSIGHTS · 句子精讲 8 U.S. customers historically tipped people they assumed were earning most of their income via tips, such as restaurant servers earning less than the minimum wage. Today's tip requests are often not connected to the salary and service norms that used to determine when and how people tip. The prevalence of digital payment devices has made it easier to ask customers for a tip. Tipping has always been a vital s...",position:0},{id:26,articleId:"text-2",type:"reading",question:"According to the first two paragraphs, the NHS ________.",options:{A:"is troubled by funding deficiencies",B:"can hardly satisfy people's needs",C:"can barely retain its current employees",D:"is rivalled by private medical services"},answer:"B",analysis:`根据前两段,NHS________。 【判型】题干 According to the first two paragraphs, the NHS ________ 要求依据指定段落补全对 NHS 现状的客观描述，没有 infer/imply/mainly about，是典型段内细节归纳。 【拆句】According to the first two paragraphs, the NHS ________. 是 "介词短语 + 主语 + 待补谓 【定位】P2 With more than 6.8 million on waitlists, healthcare is becoming increasingly inaccessible for those who cannot opt to pay for private treatment; and the cost of providing healthcare is increasingly squeezing out investment in other public services. 【选项】A) 无中生有 is troubled by funding deficiencies 用 funding deficiencies (资金短缺) 替换"难以满 B) ✓ 正确 healthcare is becoming increasingly inaccessible ↔ can hardly satisfy people's needs model is out of date ↔ can hardly satisfy people's needs 预判"需求难被满足"与 can hardly satisfy people's needs 完全同义，直接锁 B。 can barely retain its current employees 把 pressures on the workforce 偷换成"留 is rivalled by private medical services 暗示 NHS 受私营医疗的竞争挑战，原文 pay for private treatment 只是说没钱付私营治疗的人就医难，而非"NHS 被私营对手 击败"。`,position:0},{id:27,articleId:"text-2",type:"reading",question:"One answer to the crisis in health and care is to ________.",options:{A:"boost the efficiency of hospitals",B:"lighten the burden on social care",C:"increase resources for primary care",D:"reduce the pressure on communities"},answer:"C",analysis:'【判型】题干 One answer to the crisis in health and care is to ________ 是"其中一个对策是…"， 【拆句】One answer to the crisis in health and care is to ________. 是 "主语 + 系动词 + 不定式 【定位】P3 Much more needs to be invested in communities and primary care to reduce our reliance on hospitals. 【选项】A) 与原文相反 boost the efficiency of hospitals 主张提升医院效率，而原文主张"减少对医院的依 lighten the burden on social care 说"减轻社会照护负担"，原文说"capacity in social care needs to be greater"，即扩容、而非减轻其负担，方向被偷反。 C) ✓ 正确 Much more needs to be invested in communities and primary care ↔ increase resources for primary care 预判"加大初级医疗投入"与 increase resources for primary care 同向同对象，且 "resources" 是 "investment" 的上位概括，直接锁 C。 reduce the pressure on communities 把"在社区加大投入"偷换成"给社区减压"，原文',position:0},{id:28,articleId:"text-2",type:"reading",question:'"Reimagining health" is aimed to ________.',options:{A:"reinforce hospital management",B:"readjust healthcare regulations",C:"restructure the health system",D:"resume suspended health reforms"},answer:"C",analysis:'重新构想健康"旨在________。 【判型】题干 Reimagining health is aimed to ________ 问该项目的目的，是对原文显性表述的指 【拆句】"Reimagining health" is aimed to ________. 主语是项目名，谓语 is aimed to 后接动词 【定位】P4 That is why the Reform think tank is launching a new programme of work entitled "Reimagining health", supported by ten former health ministers. Together, we are calling for a much more open and honest conversation about the future of health in the UK, and an "urgent rethink" of the hospital-centric model we retain. 【选项】A) 与原文相反 reinforce hospital management 主张"强化医院管理"，原文要质疑、要 rethink 的恰 readjust healthcare regulations 把项目目的窄化为"调整医疗法规"，原文从未谈及法 C) ✓ 正确 an "urgent rethink" of the hospital-centric model we retain ↔ restructure the health system 预判"重构以医院为中心的体系"与 restructure the health system 完全同义，锁 C。 resume suspended health reforms 说"重启已搁置的改革"，原文虽提 two decades of strategies and a number of major health reforms，但只是说"未取得实质进展"，',position:0},{id:29,articleId:"text-2",type:"reading",question:"To maximise the nation's health, the author suggests ________.",options:{A:"introducing relevant taxation policies",B:"paying due attention to social factors",C:"reevaluating major health outcomes",D:"enhancing the quality of healthcare"},answer:"B",analysis:`【判型】题干 To maximise the nation's health, the author suggests ________ 问作者明确给出的 【拆句】To maximise the nation's health, the author suggests ________. To 不定式作目的状语前 置，主句 the author suggests 后接动名词宾语，选项都以 -ing 形式开头。注意 suggests 【定位】P5 Much more important are the places we live, work and socialise—yet there is no clear cross- government strategy for improving these social determinants of health. 【选项】A) 偷换概念 introducing relevant taxation policies 把原文 taxpayers are left with the heavy B) ✓ 正确 Much more important are the places we live, work and socialise ↔ paying due attention to social factors these social determinants of health ↔ paying due attention to social factors 预判"重视社会决定因素"与 paying due attention to social factors 完全同义，锁 B。 reevaluating major health outcomes 抓住原文 about 20% of health outcomes 一 未呼吁"reevaluate outcomes"。 enhancing the quality of healthcare 主张"提升医疗服务质量"，正是作者在本段开头 明确反对的"fix the NHS"路径，方向相反。`,position:0},{id:30,articleId:"text-2",type:"reading",question:"It can be inferred that local leaders should ________.",options:{A:"exercise their power more reasonably",B:"develop a stronger sense of responsibility",C:"play a bigger role in the health system",D:"understand people's health needs better"},answer:"C",analysis:`【判型】题干 It can be inferred that local leaders should ________ 含 inferred，属推断题。 【拆句】It can be inferred that local leaders should ________. 形式主语 It + can be inferred + that 从句，that 从句中 local leaders 是真主语、should 是情态动词、待补谓语。结构常 【定位】P6 What health functions should remain at the centre, and what should be given to local leaders, often responsible for services that create health, and with a much better understanding of the needs of their populations? 【选项】A) 过度推断 exercise their power more reasonably 暗指地方负责人当前行权不合理、需改正，原 develop a stronger sense of responsibility 主张地方应增强责任感，原文是"应不应该 C) ✓ 正确 what should be given to local leaders ↔ play a bigger role in the health system often responsible for services that create health, and with a much better understanding of the needs of their populations ↔ play a bigger role in the health system 预判"地方角色应扩大"与 play a bigger role in the health system 同义，符合推断 understand people's health needs better 直接复述原文 much better understanding of the needs of their populations，是原文已陈述的事实而非"应该 做"的推断；题干问 should，D 项答 is/are，错位。 SENTENCE INSIGHTS · 句子精讲 11 Nearly 75 years on, with the UK facing very different health challenges, it is clear that model is out of date. As demand for healthcare continues to grow, pressures on the workforce—which is already near breaking point—will only become more acute. We need to be much better at reducing and diverting demand on health services, rather than simply managing it. 后，学生可以迁移到类似的 "be good at / be skilled in" 等表达以及对比论证句式。不过，本句缺 Muc...`,position:0},{id:31,articleId:"text-3",type:"reading",question:"According to Paragraph 1, Nagpur's plan proposes measures to ________.",options:{A:"tackle extreme weather",B:"ensure construction quality",C:"monitor emergency warnings",D:"address excessive workloads"},answer:"A",analysis:`【判型】According to Paragraph 1, Nagpur's plan proposes measures to ________ 既限定段落 【拆句】According to Paragraph 1, Nagpur's plan proposes measures to ________ 主干 Nagpur's plan proposes measures，to 不定式接选项构成 propose measures to do 【定位】P1 Nagpur's plan, for instance, calls for hospitals to set aside "cold wards" in the summer for treating heatstroke patients, and advises builders to give construction laborers a break from work on very hot days. 【选项】A) ✓ 正确 on very hot days ↔ extreme weather treating heatstroke patients ↔ tackle 预判要的就是「应对高温」，tackle extreme weather 与两条措施全部对应，且选项里 量；ensure construction quality 把对工人的保护偷换成对工程质量的保证。 原文是 HAP 在「发布」高温警报、提醒医院，monitor emergency warnings「监控应`,position:0},{id:32,articleId:"text-3",type:"reading",question:"One problem with existing HAPs is that they ________.",options:{A:"prove too costly to be implemented",B:"lack localized alert-issuing criteria",C:"give delayed responses to heat waves",D:"keep hospitals under great pressure"},answer:"B",analysis:"【判型】One problem with existing HAPs is that they ________ 问一个具体问题，不带 infer/suggest，是细节题。 【拆句】One problem with existing HAPs is that they ________ 是 One problem is that... 的同位 结构，that 从句的主语 they 指代 existing HAPs，选项内容直接做 they 的谓语部分。结 【定位】P2 And their triggering thresholds often are not customized to the local climate. 【选项】A) 偷换概念 原文是「资金不足」（lack adequate funding），选项 prove too costly to be B) ✓ 正确 triggering thresholds ↔ alert-issuing criteria are not customized to the local climate ↔ lack localized 预判要的「预警门槛没本地化」与 lack localized alert-issuing criteria 字面对位， 原文谈的是阈值不本地化、资金不够，从未谈到响应速度；give delayed responses to heat waves 凭空引入 delayed 这一时间维度。 原文从未说现有 HAPs 给医院造成压力；keep hospitals under great pressure 借第 1 段 hospitals 的眼熟感骗考生。",position:0},{id:33,articleId:"text-3",type:"reading",question:"Mumbai's case shows that India's heat alert systems need to ________.",options:{A:"include other factors besides temperature",B:"take subtle weather changes into account",C:"prioritize potentially disastrous heat waves",D:"draw further support from local authorities"},answer:"A",analysis:"【判型】Mumbai's case shows that India's heat alert systems need to ________ 用一个具体案例 【拆句】Mumbai's case shows that India's heat alert systems need to ________ 主干 Mumbai's case shows that...，宾语从句主语是 India's heat alert systems，need to 接选项形成不 【定位】P3 But the effects of the heat were amplified by humidity—an often neglected factor in heat alert systems—and the lack of shade at the late-morning outdoor ceremony. 【选项】A) ✓ 正确 humidity—an often neglected factor in heat alert systems ↔ other factors besides temperature often neglected factor ↔ include 预判「应当纳入温度以外的因素」与 include other factors besides temperature 字面 原文说湿度等因素被忽视，并未谈天气是否「细微变化」；take subtle weather changes into account 把 neglected factor 偷换为 subtle change，方向错位。 场；prioritize potentially disastrous heat waves 凭空引入「排序」概念。 support from local authorities 凭空引入「找地方政府要资源」的话题。",position:0},{id:34,articleId:"text-3",type:"reading",question:"Kotharkar holds that a vulnerability map can help ________.",options:{A:"prevent the harm of high humidity",B:"target areas needing special attention",C:"expand the Nagpur project's coverage",D:"make relief plans for heat-stricken people"},answer:"B",analysis:"【判型】Kotharkar holds that a vulnerability map can help ________ 直接问 vulnerability map 【拆句】Kotharkar holds that a vulnerability map can help ________ 主干 Kotharkar holds that...，that 从句中 a vulnerability map can help 接选项作宾语或不定式。无陷阱。 【定位】P5 For example, neighborhoods with a large elderly population or informal dwellings that cope poorly with heat could get special warnings or be bolstered with cooling centers. The Nagpur project has already created a risk and vulnerability map, which enabled Kotharkar to tell officials which neighborhoods to focus on in the event of a heat wave this summer. 【选项】A) 张冠李戴 prevent the harm of high humidity 把 P3 中 Mumbai 段的湿度问题嫁接到 vulnerability map 的功能上；地图本身从不直接「防湿度伤害」。 B) ✓ 正确 which neighborhoods to focus on in the event of a heat wave ↔ target areas needing special attention focus responses on the populations most at risk ↔ areas needing special attention 预判「锁定该重点关注的社区」与 target areas needing special attention 在动词、对 原文 The Nagpur project has already created a risk and vulnerability map 只是说 expand the Nagpur project's coverage 把「项目里已有这张图」歪曲成「这张图去扩 划」；make relief plans for heat-stricken people 把预防/聚焦的功能改成事后救援，",position:0},{id:35,articleId:"text-3",type:"reading",question:"According to the last paragraph, researchers believe that HAPs should ________.",options:{A:"focus more on heatstroke treatment",B:"apply for more government grants",C:"invite wider public participation",D:"serve a broader range of purposes"},answer:"D",analysis:`【判型】According to the last paragraph, researchers believe that HAPs should ________ 是 「指定段 + 转述 + should」的标准细节题题干。 【拆句】According to the last paragraph, researchers believe that HAPs should ________ 主干 researchers believe that HAPs should ________，that 宾语从句的主语 HAPs 接情态动词 should，选项作动词原形短语补全 should 后内容。无陷阱。 【定位】P6 HAPs shouldn't just include short-term emergency responses, researchers say, but also recommend medium- to long-term measures that could make communities cooler. 【选项】A) 与原文相反 focus more on heatstroke treatment 与末段方向相反。 末段未提 HAPs 应去申请政府拨款；apply for more government grants 把 P2 中的 invite wider public participation 凭空引入公众参与话题。 D) ✓ 正确 shouldn't just include short-term emergency responses, researchers say, but also recommend medium- to long-term measures ↔ serve a broader range of purposes Reducing deaths in an emergency is a good target to have, but it's the lowest target ↔ a broader range of purposes serve a broader range of purposes 抓住「目标不止一种」的核心，与预判完全吻合。 SENTENCE INSIGHTS · 句子精讲 7 In general, an HAP spells out when and how officials should issue heat warnings and alert hospitals and other institutions. Nagpur's plan, for instance, calls for hospitals to set aside "cold wards" in the summer for treating heatstroke patients, and advises builders to give construction laborers a break from work on very hot days. But in other places, nighttime temperatures or humidity might be as important a gauge of risk as daytime highs. To help improve HAPs, urban planner Rajashree Kothark...`,position:0},{id:36,articleId:"text-4",type:"reading",question:"According to Paragraph 1, desire paths are a result of ________.",options:{A:"the curiosity to explore surrounding hills",B:"the necessity to preserve green spaces",C:"the tendency to pursue convenience",D:"the wish to find comfort in solitude"},answer:"C",analysis:'【判型】According to Paragraph 1, desire paths are a result of ________要求基于第一段事实给出 desire paths 形成的原因，没有 infer/imply 等推断信号，属于细节题。 【拆句】Q36主干是 desire paths are a result of ________，be a result of 表示"是……的结果"，空 格里要填的是"导致 desire paths 出现的原因"，注意方向不是"desire paths 导致了什么"。 【定位】P1 these routes of collective disobedience cut corners, bisect lawns and cross hills, representing the natural capability of people (and animals) to go from point A to point B most effectively. 【选项】A) 偷换概念 原文 cross hills 只是描述路径会"翻山"，并非出于curiosity to explore surrounding 原文说人们 bisect lawns 切草坪，正是不顾绿地保育的体现，与the necessity to preserve green spaces"为保护绿地"刚好相反。 C) ✓ 正确 to go from point A to point B most effectively ↔ the tendency to pursue convenience the natural capability of people (and animals) ↔ the tendency to the tendency to pursue convenience精确对应原文"以最高效方式从 A 到 B 的天 the wish to find comfort in solitude谈"在独处中找慰藉"，第一段完全没有 solitude/comfort 的相关表述。',position:0},{id:37,articleId:"text-4",type:"reading",question:"It can be inferred that Ohio State University ________.",options:{A:"intends to improve its desire paths",B:"leads in the research on desire paths",C:"guides the creation of its desire paths",D:"takes a positive view of desire paths"},answer:"D",analysis:'可以推断出Ohio State University 【判型】It can be inferred that Ohio State University里的 inferred 直接亮明推断题身份，要求基 【拆句】Q37结构是 It can be inferred that + 主语 + 谓语，that 引导宾语从句，主语锁死在 Ohio State University，要补出的是"学校对 desire paths 的态度或做法"。 【定位】P2 Ohio State University allowed its students to navigate the Oval, a lawn in the centre of campus, freely, then proceeded to pave the desire paths, creating a web of effective routes students had established. 【选项】A) 过度推断 原文只说学校已经把 desire paths 铺好，没说要去improve its desire paths"再改良"， leads in the research on desire paths是无中生有。 原文清楚说allowed its students to navigate...freely，路径是学生自由走出来的，不是 学校guides the creation引导的。 D) ✓ 正确 proceeded to pave the desire paths, creating a web of effective routes students had established ↔ takes a positive view of desire paths allowed its students to navigate the Oval...freely ↔ positive view takes a positive view of desire paths恰好对应学校"允许踩+主动铺路"所暗含的认可态',position:0},{id:38,articleId:"text-4",type:"reading",question:"The images on the Reddit webpage reflect ________.",options:{A:"conflicting opinions on the use of desire paths",B:"the call to upgrade the designing of public spaces",C:"the demand for proper planning of desire paths",D:"growing concerns over the loss of public spaces"},answer:"A",analysis:'【判型】The images on the Reddit webpage reflect ________问的是图所反映的客观信息，需要回 【拆句】Q38主干是 The images...reflect ________，reflect 这里是"反映/体现"，宾语是要填的内 容，介词短语 on the Reddit webpage 修饰 images。 【定位】P3 A Reddit webpage devoted to the phenomenon, boasting nearly 50,000 members, showcases images of local desire paths adorned with signs instructing pedestrians to adhere to designated walkways, underscoring the rebellious nature inherent in these human-made tracks. This clash highlights an ongoing struggle between the organic, user-driven evolution of public spaces and the desire for a visually curated and controlled urban environment. 【选项】A) ✓ 正确 This clash highlights an ongoing struggle ↔ conflicting opinions on the use of desire paths between the organic, user-driven evolution of public spaces and the desire for a visually curated and controlled urban environment ↔ on the use of desire paths conflicting opinions on the use of desire paths精准把段末总结句的双方对立概括出 原文谈的是"该不该接纳踩径"的对立，没有call to upgrade the designing of public spaces"号召升级设计"的语义。 原文反对方甚至要instructing pedestrians to adhere to designated walkways禁止走 desire paths，更谈不上demand for proper planning of desire paths要"正规规划 desire paths"。 原文焦点是"踩径与禁令的冲突"，并非growing concerns over the loss of public spaces担心公共空间"流失"。',position:0},{id:39,articleId:"text-4",type:"reading",question:"The example of the Wickquasgeck Trail illustrates ________.",options:{A:"the growth of New York City",B:"the Dutch origin of desire paths",C:"the importance of urban planning",D:"the recognition of desire paths"},answer:"D",analysis:'Wickquasgeck Trail的例子说明了 【判型】illustrates直接亮明例证题身份，问的是 Wickquasgeck Trail 这个例子在说明什么观点。 【拆句】Q39结构是 The example of X illustrates ________，主语是"X 这个例子"，谓语 illustrate 【定位】P4 The Wickquasgeck Trail is an example of a historical desire path, created by Native Americans to cross the forests of Manhattan and move between settlements quickly. Wickquasgeck Trail 是一条历史上的 desire path，由美洲原住民开辟，用以穿越曼哈顿森 【选项】A) 答非所问 原文确实顺带提到Broadway演变史，但作者举例不是为了说the growth of New York City"纽约市的成长"，纽约只是 desire path 留下的副产品。 原文明说这条路径created by Native Americans，是原住民开辟的，荷兰人只是后来拓 宽，the Dutch origin of desire paths把起源颠倒了。 整段是讲 desire paths 反过来塑造了城市的街道，恰好defies the grid-based system 违反了规划，而非证明the importance of urban planning规划的重要性。 D) ✓ 正确 The Wickquasgeck Trail is an example of a historical desire path ↔ the recognition of desire paths Broadway stands out as one of the few areas in NYC that defies the grid-based system ↔ recognition the recognition of desire paths恰是这条历史 desire path 被一代代沿用、保留至今的',position:0},{id:40,articleId:"text-4",type:"reading",question:`It can be learned from the last paragraph that desire paths ________. Part B Directions: Read the following text and answer the questions by choosing the most suitable subheading from the list A-G for each of the numbered paragraphs (41-45). There are two extra subheadings which you do not need to use. Mark your answers on the ANSWER SHEET. (10 points) Five Steps to Suggesting a Change at Work That'll Actually Get Taken Seriously Everyone wants to be that person—the one who looks at the same information as everyone else, but who sees a fresh, innovative solution. However, it takes more than simply having a good idea. How you share it is as important as the suggestion itself. Why? Because writing a new script—literally or figuratively—means that other team members will have to adapt to something new. Not to mention, if the process you're scrapping is one someone else suggested, there's the possibility of hurt feelings. To gain buy-in on an innovative, new idea, follow these steps: (41)占位符这是等待完型填空的句⼦位置 Great ideas don't stand alone. In other words, you can't mention your suggestion once and expect it to be adopted. To see a change, you'll need to champion your plan and sell its merits. In addition, you need to be willing to stand up to scrutiny and criticism and be prepared to explain your innovation in different ways for various audiences. (42)占位符这是等待完型填空的句⼦位置 Sometimes it makes sense to go to your boss first. But other times, it's useful to build a coalition among your co-workers or other stakeholders. When it works, it works great—because you're ready for your stubborn supervisor's pushback with answers like, "Actually, I connected with a few people in our tech department to discuss how much time these kinds of website updates would take, and they suggested they have the bandwidth." (43)占位符这是等待完型填空的句⼦位置 One of the biggest barriers to gaining buy-in occurs when the owner of an idea is viewed as argumentative, defensive, or close-minded. Because, let's be honest: No one likes a know-it-all. So, if people disagree with you, don't be indignant. Instead, listen to their concerns fully, try to understand their perspective, and include their concerns (and possible remedies) in future discussions. So, instead of saying, "Martha, our current slogan is confusing and should be updated," you could try, "Martha raises a great point that our current slogan has a long history for our stakeholders, but I wonder if we might be able to brainstorm a tagline that could build on that— and be clearer for new customers." (44)占位符这是等待完型填空的句⼦位置 New ideas are the grandchildren of old ones. In other words, don't throw old solutions under the bus to make your improvement stand out. Remember that in light of whatever the problem the old system solved—or, maybe, has failed to solve in recent memory—it was a great idea at the time. Appreciating the older contributions as you suggest future innovations helps bolster the credibility of your idea. (45)占位符这是等待完型填空的句⼦位置 When pitching a new idea, it's important to use the language of abundance instead of the language of deficit. Instead of saying what is wrong, broken, or suboptimal, talk about what is right, fixable, or ideal. For example, try, "I can see lots of applications for this new approach" rather than, "This innovation is the only way." Be optimistic but realistic, and you will stand out. [ E ]Invite resistance. [ F ]Be a salesman. [ G ]Be humble.`,options:{A:"Stay positive.",B:"Respect the past.",C:"Use channels.",D:"Give it time."},answer:"D",analysis:`【判型】It can be learned from the last paragraph that desire paths ________里的 learned 等价 于 inferred，是末段推断题。 【拆句】Q40主干 It can be learned...that desire paths ________，that 后是宾语从句，主语锁死 【定位】P6 Animal desire paths, such as ducks forging trails through frozen ponds or dogs carving direct routes in gardens, highlight the adaptability of these trails in both human and animal experiences. As desire paths criss-cross through both physical and virtual landscapes, they stand as a proof of the collective insistence on forging unconventional routes and embracing the spirit of communal choice. 【选项】A) 无中生有 原文反复强调的是 desire paths 体现"非常规路线"，并未涉及humans' deep respect for nature"对自然的深切尊重"。 末段没有任何crucial to humans' mental wellbeing"对心理健康至关重要"的表述，话 原文并列谈人和动物都会踩径，并未说人是在imitation of animal behaviour"模仿 动物"。 D) ✓ 正确 the adaptability of these trails in both human and animal experiences ↔ show a shared trait in humans and animals the collective insistence on forging unconventional routes and embracing the spirit of communal choice ↔ shared trait show a shared trait in humans and animals恰好把末段"both human and animal"的 并列加上"collective insistence"的总收概括成一种共有特性，与预判一致。 SENTENCE INSIGHTS · 句子精讲 9 Urban planners interpret desire paths as more than just convenient shortcuts; they offer valuable insights into the dynamics between planning and behaviour. Ohio State University allowed its students to navigate the Oval, a lawn in the centre of campus, freely, then proceeded to pave the desire paths, creating a web of effe...`,position:0}]}],o={id:e,year:2025,subject:t,title:a,sections:s};export{o as default,e as id,s as sections,t as subject,a as title,i as year};
